# Build phase 4.12. This exists so `npm ci` can succeed on Linux, and it is a
# workaround for an UPSTREAM packaging problem rather than anything wrong here.
#
# The problem, diagnosed from package-lock.json rather than guessed:
#
#   @napi-rs/wasm-runtime  peerDependencies  @emnapi/core ^2.0.0-alpha.3
#   lockfile resolves      @emnapi/core      1.11.1
#
# 1.11.1 does not satisfy ^2.0.0-alpha.3, so that peer is unsatisfiable in the
# tree as locked. It never surfaces on macOS, because `@rolldown/binding-
# wasm32-wasi` is the WASM FALLBACK and is not installed when a native darwin
# binding exists. On Linux, npm walks that branch, has to resolve the peer,
# cannot find it in the lock, and `npm ci` aborts with:
#
#   npm error Missing: @emnapi/core@2.0.0-alpha.4 from lock file
#
# So `npm ci --dry-run` passes locally and the identical lockfile fails in the
# container. The lockfile is not stale; it is platform-shaped.
#
# WHY THIS FILE RATHER THAN CHANGING THE BUILD COMMAND: Railway's Nixpacks
# builder detects a lockfile and runs `npm ci`, and that could not be
# overridden from here. `buildCommand` in `frontend/railway.json` was ignored
# (config-as-code is deprecated in favour of `.railway/railway.ts`), and
# setting `RAILWAY_BUILD_COMMAND` and `NIXPACKS_BUILD_CMD` as service variables
# did not take either. `npm` reads `.npmrc` from the project directory on every
# invocation, including `npm ci`, so this reaches the command that actually
# runs instead of fighting to replace it.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, stated rather than glossed: peer-dependency conflicts stop
# being errors and become warnings, for the whole install. That is a real
# reduction in strictness. It is scoped to this directory, the conflict it
# resolves is a genuine upstream defect (an alpha peer range against a stable
# release), and the alternative was `npm install`, which would ALSO have
# abandoned the lockfile's exact-version guarantee rather than merely its peer
# checking. Between the two, this keeps the locked versions.
legacy-peer-deps=true
